(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-pericardial-disease-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pericardial Disease Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which cancers are named for evaluation when malignancy may lie behind a pericardial effusion?","choices":["Thyroid and kidney cancer","Testicular and cervical cancer","Breast and lung cancer","Prostate and bladder cancer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The effusion lab workup includes considering an evaluation for malignancy, naming breast cancer and lung cancer as the examples. Cytology of pericardial fluid, sent when neoplastic pericarditis is strongly suspected, complements that evaluation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which etiology accounts for more than half of cases of constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Prior chest radiation","Post-cardiac surgery","Connective tissue disease","Idiopathic or viral disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. More than 50% of constrictive pericarditis cases are idiopathic or viral. The remaining causes include post-cardiac surgery, prior radiation, post-infectious disease, connective tissue disease, uremia, and prior pericarditis, trauma or systemic disease affecting the pericardium. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Besides cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery, which service may an older adult need after a prolonged illness from pericardial disease?","choices":["Nephrology","Infectious disease","Genetic counseling","Rehabilitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rehabilitation is listed with cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery among the services for older adults with pericardial disease. After a prolonged illness, and with higher morbidity and mortality from surgical interventions at older ages, recovering function is a real need. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which pericardial fluid study is rarely helpful in establishing the diagnosis?","choices":["Acid-fast stain and culture","Gram stain and culture","Cytology for malignancy","Cell count and differential"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cell count and differential are sent on pericardial fluid but are rarely helpful in establishing the diagnosis. The studies that answer specific questions are Gram stain with bacterial and fungal culture, acid-fast stain with mycobacterial culture, and cytology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"In a published African series of acute pericarditis, which cause accounted for most cases?","choices":["Tuberculosis","Idiopathic disease","Neoplastic disease","Autoimmune disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the African series, tuberculosis caused 69.5% of acute pericarditis, against under 1% in the Western European series, where idiopathic disease (55%) dominated. Where tuberculosis is common, it displaces idiopathic disease as the leading cause. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Population data suggest a pericardial effusion may be present in up to what share of the general adult population?","choices":["0.5%","6.5%","25%","40%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Data from the Framingham cohort suggest a pericardial effusion may be present in up to 6.5% of the general adult population. That contrasts with cardiac tamponade, recorded in only about 0.05% of hospital admissions. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which imaging test is now recommended for risk stratification of complicated or recurrent pericarditis?","choices":["Ventilation-perfusion scan","Plain chest radiography","Cardiac magnetic resonance","Coronary angiography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac magnetic resonance is now recommended for risk stratification of complicated or recurrent pericarditis. Edema or late gadolinium enhancement on it also identifies inflammatory, potentially reversible constriction before committing to pericardiectomy. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"After drainage, what guides further management of a pericardial effusion?","choices":["Treatment of its underlying cause","Lifelong colchicine","Routine pericardiectomy","Indefinite anticoagulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Further management of a pericardial effusion is whatever medical therapy the underlying etiology requires, for example treatment of cancer, tuberculosis or infection. Prognosis likewise depends on the underlying cause, which is why identifying it, including by studying the drained fluid, matters. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which laboratory finding is often present in constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Raised sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","A natriuretic peptide above a diagnostic cutoff","Positive blood cultures in most patients","Low albumin with heavy proteinuria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In constrictive pericarditis the inflammatory markers erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are often elevated. No B-type natriuretic peptide threshold confirms or excludes the diagnosis, although the level is likely lower than in restrictive cardiomyopathy. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"In the classic electrocardiogram evolution of acute pericarditis, what happens in stage 3?","choices":["The tracing returns fully to normal","Flattened T waves become inverted","Widespread ST elevation with PR depression","ST segments normalize and T waves flatten"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In stage 3 the flattened T waves become inverted, from about 3 weeks to several weeks. It follows stage 1 (widespread ST elevation with PR depression and reciprocal aVR changes, first 2 weeks) and stage 2 (ST normalization with T-wave flattening, 1 to 3 weeks), and precedes stage 4, a return to normal. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which chest pain pattern is classic for acute pericarditis?","choices":["Burning, worse after meals and on lying down","Sharp, worse supine, eased by leaning forward","Pressure-like, worse with exertion, eased by rest","Tearing pain that radiates through to the back"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute pericarditis typically causes pleuritic (sharp) substernal chest pain that is worse supine and during inspiration and relieved by leaning forward. The memory line: pericardial pain is pleuritic (sharp, worse with inspiration, unlike a myocardial infarction) and positional (better leaning forward). — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which is a risk factor for multiple recurrences of pericarditis?","choices":["Mild gadolinium uptake","A prompt viral recovery","An autoimmune etiology","A low C-reactive protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An autoimmune etiology is one of the three listed risk factors for multiple recurrences of pericarditis, alongside the magnitude of C-reactive protein elevation and the severity of pericardial late gadolinium enhancement. Autoimmune disease also shapes treatment, since prednisone at a second recurrence is reserved for underlying systemic autoimmune disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What adjustment is suggested for colchicine in older adults with pericarditis?","choices":["Avoid it completely","Give it intravenously","Consider dose reduction","Double the usual course"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Older adults call for caution with anti-inflammatory drugs, which sit on the Beers list, and colchicine in particular should be considered for dose reduction. The drug stays in the regimen at an adjusted dose, while corticosteroids carry their own caution because they raise osteoporosis risk. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Hoarseness with a large pericardial effusion comes from impingement of which structure?","choices":["Left phrenic nerve","Superior vena cava","Thoracic duct","Recurrent laryngeal nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Large pericardial effusions can cause dysphagia, nausea, hiccups, cough and hoarseness, and the hoarseness comes from impingement of the recurrent laryngeal nerve. These compression symptoms belong to large effusions; small ones may cause no symptoms or only vague ones. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the definitive treatment for cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Expedient pericardiocentesis","High-dose anti-inflammatories","Intravenous fluids alone","Diuresis with a loop diuretic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Expedient pericardiocentesis is the definitive treatment for cardiac tamponade. It can also be diagnostic: patients undergoing therapeutic drainage for tamponade are among those whose fluid is sent for studies such as Gram stain, cultures, acid-fast stain and cytology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"At a second recurrence, low-to-moderate dose prednisone is reserved for which patients?","choices":["Those with post-surgical injury","Those with systemic autoimmune disease","Those with a typical viral prodrome","Those with idiopathic disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. At a second recurrence, low to moderate dose prednisone is reserved for patients with an underlying systemic autoimmune disease. Idiopathic pericarditis and post-cardiac injury syndrome instead get an added interleukin-1 blocker if they are colchicine-resistant with a history of prior C-reactive protein elevation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How does a small pericardial effusion typically present?","choices":["Sharp pain eased by leaning forward","No symptoms or a vague, dull chest ache","Sudden hypotension with muffled heart sounds","Hoarseness, hiccups and dysphagia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A small pericardial effusion may be asymptomatic or cause vague symptoms: a dull ache in the chest, fatigue, decreased exercise tolerance or dyspnea. Compression symptoms appear with large effusions, and hemodynamic signs with tamponade. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which finding defines treatment failure in acute pericarditis?","choices":["A friction rub that has faded","Mild fatigue after the pain resolved","ST segments that are normalizing","A new pericardial effusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment failure is defined as persistent fever, persistent pleuritic chest pain, a new pericardial effusion or worsening general illness. Failure to respond to nonsteroidal drugs plus colchicine within 1 week suggests a cause other than idiopathic or viral pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 30"},{"q":"Which treatment addresses uremic pericarditis?","choices":["Corticosteroids","Antituberculous drugs","Pericardiectomy","Dialysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of pericarditis includes treating any underlying disorder, and for uremia that means dialysis. Anti-inflammatory therapy with nonsteroidal drugs and colchicine is the general approach, while the cause-specific step for uremic disease is dialysis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which feature marks the inflammatory phenotype of recurrent pericarditis?","choices":["Pericardial calcification","A low natriuretic peptide","C-reactive protein above 10 mg/L","A normal C-reactive protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The inflammatory phenotype is marked by a high C-reactive protein (above 10 mg/L), fever and evidence of pericardial inflammation. In such patients the 2025 guidelines support anti-interleukin-1 therapy earlier in the course, and the magnitude of C-reactive protein elevation also predicts multiple recurrences. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"After pericardiectomy for constrictive pericarditis, roughly what 5-year survival is reported?","choices":["Nearly 20%","Nearly 40%","Nearly 80%","Nearly 99%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericardiectomy increases 5-year survival to nearly 80% and 10-year survival to nearly 60%, whereas survival without treatment is low. In those who need surgery, earlier intervention is associated with improved outcomes. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What does echocardiography usually show in uncomplicated acute pericarditis?","choices":["Thickened pericardium with septal bounce","Right ventricular diastolic collapse","A ballooned apex with a normal base","Normal unless there is an effusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In uncomplicated acute pericarditis the echocardiogram is often normal unless there is an associated pericardial effusion, and the chest radiograph is typically normal. Most patients do not need invasive workup such as pericardiocentesis or pericardial biopsy. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"After a first recurrence of pericarditis, colchicine is typically continued for how long?","choices":["About 2 weeks","About 3 months","6 months or more","Lifelong therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A first recurrence is treated with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs until chest pain resolves and inflammatory markers are normal, plus colchicine for 6 months or more. That is double the 3-month colchicine course used for the initial episode. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which two groups of symptoms characterize constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Exertional syncope with a murmur","Fluid overload and low cardiac output","Pleuritic pain and a friction rub","Fever with diffuse ST elevation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Constrictive pericarditis produces symptoms of fluid overload (peripheral edema, anasarca) and/or diminished cardiac output (fatigue, dyspnea on exertion). Signs include an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign, a pericardial knock and, uncommonly, pericardial calcification. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which echocardiogram finding supports constrictive pericarditis?","choices":["Septal bounce toward the left ventricle","Right ventricular diastolic collapse","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","Apical ballooning, normal base"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Septal bounce, abnormal motion of the septum toward the left ventricle, is one of the typical echocardiographic features of constriction. The others are increased pericardial thickness, pronounced respiratory variation in ventricular filling and moderate biatrial enlargement, and together with symptoms and signs they usually make the diagnosis clinically. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"In cardiac tamponade, how does the pericardial effusion lower the cardiac output?","choices":["It mechanically impairs diastolic filling","It weakens systolic contraction directly","It blocks left ventricular outflow","It slows the heart rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In tamponade the pericardial effusion mechanically impairs diastolic filling, which decreases cardiac output. Elevated intrapericardial pressure compresses cardiac filling, producing low output, hypotension, tachycardia and oliguria. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Besides idiopathic disease, which etiology is singled out as especially important behind pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Malignancy","Coxsackievirus","Hypertension","Hyperthyroidism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beyond idiopathic disease, malignancy is singled out as an important underlying etiology, especially for pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade. In published series of moderate-to-large effusions, malignancy accounted for roughly 13 to 38% of cases. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which is the most common disorder involving the pericardium?","choices":["Acute pericarditis","Pericardial effusion","Cardiac tamponade","Constrictive pericarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute pericarditis is the most common disorder involving the pericardium. Pericardial disease presents as acute or recurrent pericarditis, as a pericardial effusion without major hemodynamic compromise, or as a compressive syndrome: tamponade, constriction or effusive-constrictive disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Most pericardial disease is labeled idiopathic. What are most idiopathic cases thought to be?","choices":["Occult malignancy","Viral infections","Autoimmune disease","Bacterial infections"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial disease is most often idiopathic, and most idiopathic cases are likely viral. Coxsackieviruses and echoviruses are typical in children, and cytomegalovirus and human immunodeficiency virus in adults. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the initial recommended therapy for most patients with acute idiopathic or viral pericarditis?","choices":["Empiric antibiotics plus prednisone","Ibuprofen or aspirin plus colchicine","Prednisone plus a proton pump inhibitor","Anakinra plus colchicine from the outset"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (aspirin, ibuprofen or indomethacin) plus colchicine are the initial recommended therapy for most acute idiopathic or viral pericarditis, with gastrointestinal protection. Prednisone takes the anti-inflammatory drug's place only when nonsteroidal drugs cannot be taken. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 28"}]};
